#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""news-image-20261124 v4 — "The world in one day: Tuesday 24 November 2026".

A single-file, stdlib-only renderer that draws a 1024x1024 newspaper front
page summing up one day's world news (35 stories, 7 region groups, 14
fields) entirely in code: vector typography, flat illustration panels, a
world map with numbered pins, a 24-story list, a date-check box and a
confidence legend.

What v4 changes against v3 (live, rank 11/7,302 in round 1, 80.27%):
  * Geneva pins 9 and 10 no longer sit in the sea. Switzerland is ~7 px
    wide on this map, so any nudge leaves it; the two Geneva stories now
    share ONE pin at the true Geneva point, labelled "09·10" (same for
    the two Seoul stories, "13·31"). The legend says so.
  * Story 31 is fixed: "Seoul: Kospi +6.3%, Nikkei +3% on Huang rally"
    (the Kospi is South Korea's index, not Tokyo's).
  * The "TO THE JUDGE:" line is gone; the date-check box is one line.
  * Four panel bodies that only repeated their headlines now carry a
    second fact, taken from another story on the same page (cross-
    references, nothing invented): the Nvidia rally lifting Seoul and
    Tokyo, the Java fire behind the Bayelsa toll, the Nairobi dateline,
    Tehran's missile threat behind the Jerusalem story.
  * The eight-more section now spans six regions, not four: Washington
    (Americas) and Jerusalem (Middle East) join as panels 6 and 8; the
    Java fire and the Hobart stadium move to the list. Region totals
    are unchanged by the swap (counts are over all 35 stories).
  * The 24-story list is two columns instead of three, so entries get
    ~490 px of air each instead of ~322 px.
  * Story 29 is corrected to its real venue: the India v New Zealand
    first Test was played in Wellington, New Zealand (Basin Reserve),
    so its region is Oceania, not Asia. Region counts become
    6+6+11+3+4+4+1 = 35; the legend is computed from the data, so it
    still matches the map.
  * Map pins are zero-padded like every other number on the page
    ("09", not "9"), and pin nudges are re-checked one by one: every
    nudged pin still sits on real land (the Gulf pin marks the Gulf
    itself, which is water by definition) and no two pins overlap.
  * Lead 3 (COP31) is reframed as a Tuesday development: the collapse
    was Sunday, but Tuesday's news is the blame-trading that followed
    (Erdogan's groundwork claim, the EU's refusal of a weak text).
    No stronger standalone Tuesday lead exists among the Europe stories
    without breaking the region counts, so the story stays a lead.

Kept from v3: serif display + clean sans (no pixel font), 35 stories,
14 fields, DATE CHECK + UTC timeline + confidence badges, byte-identical
determinism, ~2 s runtime, stdlib only, no network/exec/eval, MIT.

Deterministic: no randomness, no clock, no network, no file reads at
render time. Two runs produce byte-identical output.

Output location: an explicit path argument wins; otherwise the
MAKEFUN_OUTPUT_DIR environment variable (the grader runs on a read-only
checkout with that variable set), and failing that output/entry.png next
to this file.

Usage:  python3 main.py [output-path]     (default: output/entry.png)
"""

